Fix ppc64le platform metadata: host detection and constraint value.

rules_python 2.2.0's PLATFORMS entry for `ppc64le-unknown-linux-gnu`
has two problems on ppc64le, both stemming from using the legacy "ppc"
identifiers:

1. `arch = "ppc"` — but `repo_utils.get_platforms_cpu_name()` returns
   "ppc64le" on ppc64le hosts. `_is_compatible_with_host()` compares
   the two strings for equality, so no runtime is ever considered
   host-compatible on ppc64le, even though the bundled runtimes
   manifest carries ppc64le entries for every recent CPython version.
   As a result the `python_<version>_host` repo is not generated, and
   any module that imports it (e.g. rules_pycross 0.8.3's
   `use_repo(python, "python_3_12_host")`) fails module resolution:

     module extension "python" ... does not generate repository
     "python_3_12_host", yet it is imported as "python_3_12_host" ...

2. `compatible_with = [..., "@platforms//cpu:ppc"]` — this list is
   consumed downstream as config_setting constraint_values (e.g.
   rules_pycross's `environments.create_for_python_toolchains` bakes
   it into the per-environment config_settings that gate every wheel
   select()). Platform definitions that declare ppc64le targets use
   `@platforms//cpu:ppc64le`, so those config_settings can never
   match, failing analysis of anything depending on a hub package:

     configurable attribute "actual" in @@..//_lock:_wheel_pyyaml@6.0.3
     doesn't match this configuration. Would a default condition help?

The sibling `s390x-unknown-linux-gnu` entry already uses the matching
`arch = "s390x"` / `@platforms//cpu:s390x` pair, which is why s390x
hosts are unaffected by either problem. Align ppc64le the same way.

Applied via `single_version_override(patches=...)` on rules_python in
//MODULE.bazel. Candidate for upstreaming to bazel-contrib/rules_python.

--- a/python/versions.bzl	2026-07-22 11:22:02
+++ b/python/versions.bzl	2026-07-24 12:24:57
@@ -115,15 +115,26 @@
             arch = "x86_32",
         ),
         "ppc64le-unknown-linux-gnu": platform_info(
+            # @platforms//cpu:ppc64le (not the legacy :ppc): this list is
+            # what downstream tooling (e.g. rules_pycross target
+            # environments) uses as config_setting constraint_values, and
+            # platform definitions that declare ppc64le hosts/targets use
+            # the ppc64le constraint. With :ppc here, those settings can
+            # never match on ppc64le target platforms.
             compatible_with = [
                 "@platforms//os:linux",
-                "@platforms//cpu:ppc",
+                "@platforms//cpu:ppc64le",
             ],
             target_settings = [
                 is_libc_glibc,
             ],
             os_name = LINUX_NAME,
-            arch = "ppc",
+            # Must match repo_utils.get_platforms_cpu_name(), which returns
+            # "ppc64le" on ppc64le hosts. With "ppc" here,
+            # _is_compatible_with_host() never matches on ppc64le, so no
+            # python_*_host repo is generated for any version even though
+            # the runtime manifest carries ppc64le entries.
+            arch = "ppc64le",
         ),
